# EXP task: finite-sample study of coupled IV gradient updates

## Research question
For the fixed synthetic data generator below, how do the two learning rates affect the finite-iteration distance of the coefficient iterate from the sample 2SLS solution, and how does coefficient error against the generated true beta vary with sample size? This is a CPU numerical study only; it does not test Transformer performance.

## Fixed objects and method
Use real-valued float64 arrays with Z in R^(n x q), X in R^(n x p), Y in R^n. No intercept. Define Theta_hat = argmin_T ||Z T - X||_F^2 and beta_2SLS = argmin_b ||Z Theta_hat b - Y||_2^2, using numpy.linalg.lstsq with rcond=None. The starter supplies generator and controls. Require full column rank of Z and Z Theta_hat for the primary experiment; record and replace rank-deficient generated replicates using a predeclared replacement-seed rule or report them as failures (do not silently drop).

Initialize beta^(0)=0 in R^p and Theta^(0)=0 in R^(q x p). For each step, from a frozen old state, compute both:

Theta^(t+1) = Theta^(t) - eta Z^T (Z Theta^(t) - X)
beta^(t+1) = beta^(t) - alpha Theta^(t)^T Z^T (Z Theta^(t) beta^(t) - Y).

This is the ordering in paper equations (8a)–(8b), with beta RHS also evaluated at old Theta^(t). Do not divide gradients by n. The paper gives sufficient bounds 0 < alpha < 2 / sigma_max^2(Z Theta_hat) and 0 < eta < 2 / sigma_max^2(Z); calculate and record these per dataset. For selectable stable settings, choose a declared fraction of each bound (strictly between 0 and 1); for outside-bound settings select at least one positive rate beyond a bound. The bound is sufficient-condition guidance, not a promise that every finite coupled trajectory appears stable. State exact fractions/rates and do not tune against hidden results.

## Fixed synthetic data object
For each independent replicate, use a recorded NumPy Generator seed and draw in this order: Z (n x q) iid N(0,1); true Theta (q x p) iid N(0,1); true beta (p) iid N(0,1); Phi (p x p) iid N(0,1); phi (p) iid N(0,1); U (n x p) iid N(0,1); E2 (n x p) iid N(0,1); E1 (n) iid N(0,1). Set noise=1, X=Z Theta + noise U Phi + E2, and Y=X beta + noise U phi + E1. Fixed base dimensions p=5, q=10 and sample sizes n in {50,100,150}; repetitions and seed schedule are experimenter-selected and must be disclosed. This generator is an explicitly scoped simulation design; conclusions apply only to it.

## Hypotheses (test, do not assume)
H1: For declared stable learning-rate settings, ||beta^(t)-beta_2SLS||_2 moves toward the declared numerical tolerance over the iteration budget on full-rank samples.
H2: The distance trajectory depends on both alpha and eta; at least one tested setting outside the sufficient bound fails to reach the same tolerance within the fixed budget. Report if this does not occur.
H3: Finite-iteration ||beta^(t)-beta||_2 need not be lower than the corresponding 2SLS parameter error; measure both across n without inferring H3 from H1.

## Controls and metrics
Report OLS, 2SLS, and coupled-update coefficient errors ||b-beta||_2. Record per iteration ||beta^(t)-beta_2SLS||_2, finite-iteration ||beta^(t)-beta||_2, parameter error for controls, rank/condition diagnostics, non-finite values, and stopping/failure status. Predeclare an iteration budget and tolerance. Every aggregate must identify its independent replicate count and aggregation (e.g. mean and median); retain per-replicate values. Do not fabricate confidence intervals or claim significance from rounded aggregates. Numerical error is not statistical uncertainty.

## Required submission
Provide runnable code, machine-readable configuration and per-replicate results, plus a hypothesis-to-experiment/evidence map and concise conclusions with uncertainty and limitations. No separate prose research report. Include versions, dtype, dimensions, seeds, step-size protocol, tolerance, iteration budget, rank handling, metrics and solver status. The conclusion may be negative or inconclusive.

## Input and boundary
Inputs are this task and `base_resources.py`; work offline in the pinned CPU environment. The supplied base resource provides generation and OLS/2SLS controls, and does not implement the coupled iteration. Build and validate the experiment independently. Do not claim Transformer or GPU performance from this task.

